WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.601
- [Presenter] Revolving doors
always open, always closed.

00:00:04.601 --> 00:00:06.504
So, why do we have them?

00:00:06.504 --> 00:00:08.793
(bouncy music)

00:00:08.793 --> 00:00:11.033
Meet Theophilus Van Kannel.

00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:13.437
A curmudgeon and a scrooge of sorts.

00:00:13.437 --> 00:00:14.579
- Humbug!

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:16.334
- [Presenter] He hated lots of things.

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:19.292
None more so than chivalry,
especially opening doors

00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:20.954
for women.

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:24.055
He also hated and I mean
hated that awkward dance

00:00:24.055 --> 00:00:25.421
we've all experienced.

00:00:25.421 --> 00:00:27.194
- After you.
- No, after you.

00:00:27.194 --> 00:00:28.721
- [Man] No, no, no, I insist after you.

00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:30.446
- [Second Man] After you.

00:00:30.446 --> 00:00:32.276
- [Presenter] Van Kannel
created a way to eliminate

00:00:32.276 --> 00:00:34.446
his social phobia by drawing inspiration

00:00:34.446 --> 00:00:36.163
from a preexisting German,

00:00:36.163 --> 00:00:37.587
(speaking in foreign language).

00:00:37.587 --> 00:00:40.194
Translation: door without draft of air.

00:00:40.194 --> 00:00:42.556
In 1888, Van Kannel
received a patent for a

00:00:42.556 --> 00:00:45.896
storm door structure
aka the revolving door.

00:00:45.896 --> 00:00:49.153
In 1899, the first one
was installed at Rector's,

00:00:49.153 --> 00:00:50.821
a restaurant in New York City.

00:00:50.821 --> 00:00:52.533
It was revolutionary!

00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:55.498
Not only did it eliminate
forced social interaction,

00:00:55.498 --> 00:00:58.331
revolving doors kept
dust, rain, sleet, snow,

00:00:58.331 --> 00:01:00.865
and noise from entering
buildings and kept the heat

00:01:00.865 --> 00:01:02.552
and air conditioning inside.

00:01:02.552 --> 00:01:05.766
Seems great, energy efficient,
but there's a catch.

00:01:05.766 --> 00:01:07.358
No one uses them.

00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:11.186
In 2006, an MIT study found
only 20% to 30% of people

00:01:11.186 --> 00:01:13.219
were using the revolving doors.

00:01:13.219 --> 00:01:14.974
So, why don't we use them?

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:17.535
Probably because swinging
doors are just easier,

00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:19.488
but Mr. Van Kannel, we salute you

00:01:19.488 --> 00:01:21.279
and your anti-social ways.

00:01:21.279 --> 00:01:24.196
(tambourine music)

